[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Heat pump water heaters have been around for over a decade. If you follow this channel, you probably know they\'re three to five times more efficient than a standard electric tank. Maybe you\'ve got one. I do. But here\'s what most people don\'t realize. Even if you own a heat pump water heater, it\'s still pretty'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dumb. It heats reactively. The tank drops below the set point and the compressor kicks on. It doesn\'t know your schedule. It doesn\'t know your electricity rates &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and it definitely doesn\'t know when your solar panels are cranking. Two Boston'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'startups think that this is a massive missed opportunity. They\'re treating the water heater as a thermal battery, a cheap [music] insulated vessel that can store energy as heat, shift electricity used to cheaper hours, and participate'

[00:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in grid services. One of them, Wilmington, Massachusetts [music] based Caleffi Systems, invited me to tour their factory. Their predictive software is licensed from the National Renewable Energy Lab. According to a California Energy Commission study,'

[00:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'intelligent scheduling on time of use rate plans alone can cut cost by [music] 15 to 20% beyond what a standard heat pump water heater already saves. So I went inside to find out, &gt;&gt; [music]'

[01:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; is a smart water heater actually worth the premium or is intelligent just a buzzword bumper sticker slapped on a tank of hot water? I\'m Matt Ferrell. Welcome to Undecided. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[01:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; This video is brought to you by Ground News. Caleffi Systems was founded in 2020. They started with 100 [music] square feet at Greentown Labs, which is the climate tech incubator in Somerville, Massachusetts. Now they\'re in a 25,000 square foot manufacturing and R&amp;D facility and'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they\'re shipping product. I met up with Mike Ting, the VP of hardware and co-founder, who walked me through the operation. Mike\'s background is in consumer hardware. He spent years at Bose on their headphone team and then at Shark'

[01:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Ninja on Ninja kitchen products. His co-founder Michael Rigney is a mechanical engineer with 20 years in the electricity market. That includes a stint at EnerNOC, the demand response company that IPO\'d in 2007. Between them, they\'ve got the hardware chops and'

[01:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the energy market knowledge to pull this off. But before we got into how the factory works, I wanted to understand what makes this water heater different from the Rheem or A. O. Smith heat pump units that you can buy at Home Depot. Because on the surface, it\'s still just'

[02:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a tank that heats water. So what\'s actually changed? I sat down with Michael Rigney to get to the core of the issue. Yeah, so there\'s [music] there\'s two fundamental fundamental ways to think about how, you'

[02:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'know, Caleffi heat pump water heater saves money versus a different heat pump water heater. So the first is with the variable speed compressor, we run at a lower speed and that increases [music]'

[02:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the efficiency of the heat transfer process. That\'s number one. Right. Second, by preheating the tank, we\'re able [music] to avoid the use of the electric resistance elements, right? And and that is a tremendous use of energy.'

[02:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The third way, a typical heat pump water heater is going to reheat as soon as it\'s cold. Well, you don\'t need to do that if it\'s 9:00 and no one is going to use hot water until 7:00 in the morning.'

[02:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'We\'re able to identify that, we\'re able to avoid some of that heating and that gives us increased [music] efficiency as well. Finally, some units are installed in a garage or an attic where the'

[03:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'temperature changes throughout the day. And so if we\'re able to coordinate the heating with warmer air, you get a really, you know, nice efficiency boost from that as well. So that\'s four ways to squeeze efficiency out of a heat pump'

[03:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'water heater without changing any external infrastructure. Just by being smarter about when and how you heat. So let me translate those. A variable speed compressor is like driving 55 instead of 80. You get better mileage. Predictive'

[03:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'preheating means the resistant backup elements almost never fire. One field study found that older GE units burn nearly a third of their electricity on resistance backup. Every time those elements kick in, you\'re'

[03:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'basically running an old school electric heater. One thing I find so frustrating about my Rheem heat pump water heater is that it has an energy saver mode that you would think will save you energy. The app has a giant banner yelling at me'

[03:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to change into that mode every time I look at it. But in my testing, the energy saver actually uses more energy, not less. It\'s because it\'s kicking on the resistance heating elements after we use hot water to speed up the hot water'

[04:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recovery time. If I leave my unit in heat pump only mode, it uses [music] a lot less energy overall. I also talked to Reed Korman about his experience with a smart heat pump water heater. Reed\'s been a moderator on my'

[04:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Discord channel for years and by pure coincidence, [music] he\'s also one of the first people in the country to own a Caleffi unit. He swapped out a natural gas water heater and has been tracking the data ever since. His take on the day to day? I\'m a data nerd, so I go in the'

[04:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'app every other day and I look at the charts, but I don\'t actually change any settings. It just it just works. And if you get low, like maybe less than a third of tank of hot water, it goes into boost mode and it recovers it and'

[04:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything\'s fine. It just I don\'t have to change any settings. That\'s the contrast. My Rheem screams at me every time I open the app. Reed doesn\'t touch a thing. For Caleffi, skipping unnecessary reheating means if nobody'

[04:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'needs hot water until 7:00 a.m., the system lets the tank coast and reheats at 5:00 a.m. when electricity might be cheaper. And ambient temperature coordination means if the unit is in a garage that hits 75° Fahrenheit in the afternoon, it runs the compressor during'

[05:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the warm hours. Simple physics, but no standard heat pump water heater does this. And that\'s before you add solar or time of use rates. I know we talked about this before'

[05:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'already, but like could you just describe how [music] thinking about a water heater as a battery changes the approach to the design of a water heater? Yeah, so so my background is is first, I\'m a mechanical engineer with a'

[05:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'background in the thermal sciences. The thermal sciences is just basically how do heat and energy behave in systems, right? That\'s all it really is. That\'s first. And then I\'ve spent 20 years working in the electricity, you know, market overall, primarily in demand'

[05:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'response and energy efficiency. If there is one thing you learn working in the in those fields is that when electricity is used has value. Now, I also came into the space um by encountering the'

[06:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'predictive control [music] technology that we ultimately licensed from from the National Renewable Energy Lab. And if you have that background, it\'s just like this light bulb moment where you\'re like, of course that\'s better because it maximizes shiftability. And you know,'

[06:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shiftability is is that capability to change in time when the electricity is used and that is the fundamental driver of the design that we have.'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Shiftability, [music] that\'s the word. A regular water heater heats reactively. The tank gets cold, it turns on. Caleffi System heats predictively. It knows when you\'ll need hot water and pre-builds it into when the conditions are most'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'favorable. And the software decides the when. But here\'s the hardware trick that makes the whole thermal battery concept click. It\'s the mixing valve. A traditional water heater stores hot water at a temperature that you want to use it,'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'maybe 125° Fahrenheit. Caleffi System stores water hotter than that. It\'s overcharging the battery. You\'re packing in more thermal energy than you need right now, so you can draw it down later. The obvious problem? Nobody wants scalding water from the faucet. The'

[07:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mixing valve solves that. As hot water leaves the tank, the valve blends in cold water to hit exactly the right temperature that the homeowner prefers. Perfectly tempered every time. And here\'s the practical upside. A 65'

[07:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'gallon tank full of extra hot water mixed down on the way out delivers far more usable hot water than 65 gallons at normal set points. You effectively get a bigger tank without a bigger tank. So the insulation holds the heat, the'

[07:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mixing valve meters it out at the right temperature, and software decides when to charge. That\'s what makes this a thermal battery. You charge when electricity is cheapest or when solar is producing, you discharge the battery whenever someone turns on the tap. The'

[07:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mixing valve and the software are the pieces that connect the energy strategy to the everyday experience of just &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; having hot water. Reed\'s household put that to the test. The tank is 65 gallons, but it\'s big enough to have a little bit of a buffer.'

[08:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I have six people in my house and we never run out of hot water. Six people and it never runs out. That\'s the mixing valve and the software doing its job. But I wanted to see how this actually gets built because if the intelligence depends on precise hardware, the'

[08:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'manufacturing has to match. But before the factory tour, a quick primer for anyone new to heat pump water heaters. Mike had the perfect way to explain it. Then when people realize, oh, it\'s the same basic principle as why an air'

[08:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'conditioner takes heat from inside my house and moves it outside, they go, oh, that makes sense. Yeah. Right? So instead of toasting the water or instead of burning a fossil fuel to heat the water, you\'re using the electricity to power the compressor, which is just a pump. And so you get efficiencies that'

[08:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are three to five times higher than those systems. That\'s the fundamental difference. A traditional electric water heater converts electricity directly into heat. A heat pump moves heat from the surrounding air into the water. It\'s the same principle as your fridge'

[08:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'running in reverse. Three to five times less energy for the same hot water. So that\'s the physics. But claims about how [music] much these things actually save you? Well, they vary wildly depending on where you read about them.'

[09:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Revolutionary breakthrough or overhyped greentech? Same product, completely different narratives. I went inside the factory, talked to the engineers, and dug into the real numbers to find out if the smart premium is actually worth it. But first, let me show you a tool that I'

[09:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use to cut through that noise. This is where today\'s sponsor, Ground News, comes in. Created by a former NASA engineer, Ground News pulls from over 50,000 sources and breaks down political bias, credibility, ownership, and even financial incentives behind the'

[09:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coverage. A great example, take any major story about renewable energy policy like this one about how the UK is responding to the energy price spike caused by the Iran war. With one click, I can see a summary, political bias,'

[09:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ownership details, and a factuality breakdown for every outlet covering it. The center-leading source keeps it straightforward and highlights how the UK Prime Minister is drawing up plans to help lower bills. The left-leaning headline focuses on the clean tech angle'

[09:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and possibility of zero bills. Meanwhile, a right-leaning source hits on how the war fallout is forcing the UK towards mandates. Same story, three completely different narratives. If you\'re watching my channel, you probably'

[10:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like digging deeper into the science and technology behind these stories. Ground News helps you compare coverage, spot bias, and catch what others might miss. I especially like the blind spot feed. It shows stories underreported by one side of the spectrum. It\'s helped me'

[10:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'recognize my own blind spots and understand the nuance behind the headlines. For a limited time, you can get the same plan that I use for nearly half off. Just head to ground.news/undecided or scan the QR code to save 40% off'

[10:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'their Vantage plan. Thanks to Ground News and to all of you for supporting the channel. Now, I promised I\'d show you what\'s actually inside these things. So, Mike took me through the entire build process. Okay. So, basically what happens is we start and'

[10:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'we get a raw tank. Uh the tank comes in. The first thing we do is we bring it over here and this is a stud welding station. So, there\'s a gun over here that basically is a capacitive [music] discharge gun and we put threaded studs in there and weld them on various'

[11:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'locations in the tank and those studs are then used to hold brackets which hold various parts on. From there, the tank moves to a rotating pallet where they thermal grease the walls. Think of it like thermal paste on a CPU. Improves the heat transfer through the carbon'

[11:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'steel. Every manufacturer does this in some [music] form. Kella had to figure out their own process from scratch. We literally started by buying, you know, buckets of this stuff. It\'s sort of like peanut butter. Smearing it on, which is really inconsistent. And then, from'

[11:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there, it was like, "Okay, now how do we actually develop a process for manufacturing?" &gt;&gt; Now, they\'ve got a dialed-in method that gives every tank the same thin, even coating. Consistent and repeatable. Next comes the part that actually makes this a heat pump. But, basically the the'

[11:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'aluminum coil wraps around the tank and this is what the refrigerant flows through, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; right? So, you have your refrigerant, which is a fluid really good at absorbing heat. Um that gets sent around this loop by the compressor, which is just a pump. And'

[11:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so, it flows in through the top, down around the bottom, and that\'s what transfers heat into the tank, right? And so, this is where you\'ve got aluminum coil, you\'ve got the thermal grease, so you\'ve got really good heat conductivity through here. So, that\'s where you\'re'

[12:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'getting your efficiency gains is by using that heat pump instead of using the electric elements. At this stage, it\'s basically just an electric water heater. Elements, wiring, control boards, but nothing smart yet. The heat pump components come after insulation.'

[12:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The tank moves to the forming station where they inject liquid polyurethane, which is the same stuff that might be in your home\'s walls. This is the step that makes the thermal battery concept possible. 2 in of polyurethane foam means that the tank holds heat extremely'

[12:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'well. You can heat the water hours, even half a day in advance and barely lose anything. The foaming process was a challenge to master. It turns out foam wasn\'t built in a day. This operation is superficially really easy cuz it\'s just'

[12:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'like hold the foaming gun and shoot, but actually dialing in the recipe for how much foam you want, making sure that you get good packing without having foam sort of shooting out is actually very challenging. The liquid foam is the key. Unlike spray foam, it flows around the'

[12:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tank and fills in every gap before it cures. But, 90 seconds later, you\'ve got a perfectly insulated shell. From there, final assembly starts and everything on the top of the tank, the heat pump components, [music] they all go on next.'

[13:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Brazing is where the refrigerant lines get joined and Kella uses induction brazing instead of open flame. So, every joint gets the exact same amount of heat. It\'s more repeatable and more reliable. They leak test with a helium sniffer, which is not a special breed of'

[13:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'dog, but a specific tool. We actually fill the system and we use this little sniffer gun, sniff around all the joints and make sure there\'s no micro leaks that would wouldn\'t show up just from auditory or um, you know, a a grosser measurement of of'

[13:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pressure. And so, again, that helium sniffer can detect like a couple grams over the course of a year. So, it\'s really trying to make sure that the heat pump here is is bulletproof. For a product that\'s supposed to last 15 to 20 years in your basement, that kind of precision matters. And the refrigerant'

[13:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they\'re using is R513A, which has about half the global warming potential of R134A, which is in most competitors. And then, the final thing we do is is um a safety check called a a hipot test and'

[13:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s a high potential test. So, we apply about 2,000 V for 60 seconds and that\'s to make sure there\'s no breakdown. Um once that passes, we then power the unit on and we run our own internal diagnostic test. So, we actually turn'

[14:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the compressor on, make sure heat\'s getting into the tank. It runs through some software checks to make sure all the sensors are reading, make sure it\'s able to connect to Wi-Fi. Once a unit ships and gets installed, Kella can see when it comes online and they monitor'

[14:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the new installations for the first few days. As I mentioned earlier, we have an internal alerting system where if any one of a set of abnormal conditions is triggered in a unit, we get alerted. We\'ll go take a look at the data. Most of the time, it\'s just a false alarm,'

[14:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but we are very conservative and would rather be safe than sorry. If there does happen to be an issue, we can pinpoint what the issue is and we can either say, "Hey, we\'re going to reach out to the homeowner directly. They can we can walk them through how to fix it." Or for whatever reason we do need to send an installer out, we could say, "This is'

[14:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'exactly what\'s wrong. This is the part you\'re going to need." The ability to remotely diagnose a unit before a technician shows up is a legitimate advantage. No wasted truck rolls, no guessing. The installer knows exactly what they\'re walking into. I really wish my water heater had this part of the'

[15:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system. Reed heard the same thing from his installer. And he said back to me, he said, "These are one of the highest quality water heaters we\'ve ever installed and we\'d love to install more of them because they\'re just great to work'

[15:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with." That kind of feedback matters. Installers recommend the brands that are easy to work with. If the people putting these in are impressed, that\'s a good sign. So, the manufacturing is solid. The intelligence is real. Remote monitoring is a genuine differentiator.'

[15:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But now comes the question I\'ve been building towards. Does all of this actually pay for itself? Do all the bells and whistles make dollars and cents? The whole point of a [music] smart heat'

[15:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pump water heater is that it saves you more money than a regular one. So, how much more does it actually save? Well, it depends. 46% of American households have electric resistance water heaters, right? 6% have oil and propane and then'

[15:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'roughly 47% the balance have natural gas. And the reason I bring that up and it\'s just not a short answer is it depends on what you have, right? Cuz the savings is going to be a function of'

[16:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'where you are cuz the price of electricity and the price of natural gas is massively different in different parts across the country. I dug into the research because the numbers tell a more nuanced story than any marketing pitch. The biggest chunk of savings comes from'

[16:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the heat pump itself. Energy Star estimates a family of four saves roughly $550 a year switching from electric resistance to a heat pump water heater. That accounts for the vast majority of the savings. Smart or not, that\'s the'

[16:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'math. Your electricity rate will determine exactly how much. On top of that, smart controls earn their keep through time-of-use optimization. A California Energy Commission study found 15 to 20% cost savings from intelligent'

[16:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scheduling on time-of-use rate plans. That translates to roughly $70 per year on a standard time-of-use rate with a potential of $700 in a lifetime value. But, there\'s a catch. Storing water'

[16:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hotter drops efficiency from about 2.8 COP to 1.8. The mixing valve partially offsets that, letting the tank deliver more usable hot water even at higher set points. The bigger the gap between your peak and off-peak rates, the more that'

[17:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'trade-off pays off. Then there\'s the demand response payments. Utilities pay homeowners $10 to $110 per year to allow load shifting events. A Department of Energy-backed field study across 250'

[17:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'water heaters in 10 utilities showed twice-daily load shifts with only a 4% opt-out rate. In fact, that study found that connected heat pump water heaters cut 90% of evening peak load compared to'

[17:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'standard electric resistance units. And remember that field study where older GE units lost nearly a third of their energy to resistance backup? Smart scheduling avoids that. Another $20 a year. It\'s not huge on its own, but it'

[17:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'stacks. Add it all up, a smart heat pump water heater replacing an electric resistance saves roughly $630 to $810 but only $100 to $310 of that comes from'

[17:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the smart features. The rest comes from the heat pump. And for Kella, the unit costs $2,999 before rebates. Michael says that\'s cost-competitive with comparable heat'

[18:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pump water heaters in the professional installer channel. So, the biggest savings come from replacing electric resistance in warm climates with steep time-of-use rates. The smallest from replacing gas in a cold climate on a flat electricity rate. But even there,'

[18:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'there are still savings. And then, there\'s the comfort angle. There\'s nothing smart about a water heater if you\'re taking cold showers. Comfort matters. It\'s like ample hot water at times of peak demand. Like,'

[18:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s what people buy the water heater for. And so, how homeowners really think about this is is, "Hey, yeah, I\'m getting savings." But, they also think about it is, "I have greater confidence that I\'m going to have ample hot water'

[18:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'when my family wants it, right?" You know, the last person to take a shower gets a hot shower, too. &gt;&gt; So, the honest assessment? The economics work, but they\'re layered. If someone on social media tells you a smart water heater will save you thousands, they\'re right about the heat pump part. The'

[19:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'smart premium, though, that depends on your rate structure, your solar setup, and whether your utility has demand response programs. It\'s real savings, not life-changing savings. And Kella isn\'t the only company trying to raise'

[19:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the IQ on heat pump water heaters. Another Boston area startup, Reservoir, is taking the opposite approach. Less software, more hardware. Their 50-gallon unit packs in a recirculation pump, ultrasonic leak detection, an electronic'

[19:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anode rod rated for 20 years, and four independent heating elements. They claim a UEF of 3.53 and a virtual 150-gallon capacity. Their units cost about $6,500 [music]'

[19:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and are fully installed by their own crews and are currently limited to the Boston metro area. So, the philosophical split is clear. [music] Kella is intelligence first, sophisticated predictive software paired with targeted hardware upgrades. Yeah. And Reservoir'

[19:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is a hardware-first commercial grade components in a consumer product. It\'s different bets, but the same thermal battery idea. Both treat the water heater as a thermal battery. Both are in Boston.'

[20:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The big story here isn\'t about which company wins, is that the water heater, an appliance most of us never think about, is becoming a smart grid-connected device. One smart design choice that Caleffi does that jumped out at me was not'

[20:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'needing to a direct connection to your solar inverter. You tell it about which system you have, which direction the panels in your house face, your [music] address, and it pulls the solar forecast. It works with any system from day one. Direct integrations add'

[20:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'precision on top, but the baseline coordination is already there. Reed confirmed that\'s how it works in practice. Yeah, I have a small solar setup that only covers, you know, maybe 50% of my energy usage, but I gave them the specs on my solar setup by'

[20:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'uploading like my Con Ed bill, and you can see the the energy usage during the day. Like when it when it\'s real sunny, it bumps up the heat generation, and it it\'s really cool. A modest solar'

[20:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'setup, no special wiring, the system just figures it out. If that\'s not smart, I don\'t know what is. It also announced Home Assistant support, which for the smart home crowd opens the door to connecting to pretty'

[21:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'much anything. And they\'re heading towards Matter, which is the emerging smart home standard, which should unlock a wave of integrations all at once. So, after spending the day walking through the facility, watching every step from raw tank to finished product, talking to'

[21:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the people who designed and built it, here\'s my take. The biggest win is still the heat pump itself. Switching from electric resistance to any heat pump water heater saves you about $550 a year or more. That\'s the dominant move. Smart'

[21:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or not, that\'s the math. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Stack those up, and the intelligence adds another $100 to $310 in savings per year. Meaningful, [music] but incremental. It earns its keep if you have solar, if'

